        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, idiomatic clone of the validation patterns already established in this codebase, and everything lands in the right unit.\n\n- **Function** `EnsureRegistrationDateNotFuture` mirrors the existing `Ensure*` validators (`EnsurePostcodeValidForCity`, `EnsureDailyRegistrationLimit`): a single `service` method reading `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, throwing a checked escalation. No logic that belongs elsewhere.\n- **Exception** `FutureRegistrationDateException` matches `InvalidPostcodeException`/`BlankAddressException` exactly \u2014 checked, carries a `rejectedValue` with a getter, message built in the constructor, Javadoc pointing at its handler.\n- **Handler** reuses the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper and follows the `field`/`rejectedValue` property convention verbatim from `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` and `BlankAddressExceptionHandler`. Nothing is duplicated that should have been shared.\n- **YAML** escalation registration and the `next:` wiring into `owners.POST.yml` follow the documented conventions.\n- **Pipeline placement** is correct and thoughtful: `ensureRegistrationDateNotFuture` runs *before* `resolveRegistrationDate`, so it inspects the raw supplied value (via `OwnerFieldsDto.getRegistrationDate()`) rather than the server-defaulted/business-day-rolled value \u2014 exactly matching the \"validated only when present\" contract described in both classes' Javadoc and the `ResolveRegistrationDate` default behavior.\n\nNo class or method is becoming a catch-all; a maintainer would find this indistinguishable from the surrounding code."
